title: GSocket gs-netcat Client Execution on Linux Host
id: d0dc10ec-6fb8-4c5c-a060-560c45202cf3
status: experimental
description: >
    Detects execution of the GSocket relay client (gsocket / gs-netcat) on a
    Linux host. Microsoft Security Research reported unrelated threat actors
    deploying GSocket after web-application RCE to keep an encrypted,
    NAT-traversing channel that outlives patching; the tool hides behind
    kernel-thread-like process names and persists through cron, systemd units
    and shell startup files. GSocket is legitimate open-source software, so a
    hit is a triage lead, not proof of compromise - it matters on servers that
    have no business running a relay-based remote access tool.
references:
    - https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/11/11/global-socket-how-threat-actors-weaponizing-gsocket-stealthy-c2/
    - https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/hackers-plant-gsocket-on-linux-servers-for-persistent-access/
author: Tia N. List
date: 2026/11/12
tags:
    - attack.t1219
    - attack.t1090
    - attack.t1036
logsource:
    category: process_creation
    product: linux
detection:
    selection_binary:
        Image|endswith:
            - '/gs-netcat'
            - '/gsocket'
            - '/gsnc'
    selection_commandline:
        CommandLine|contains:
            - 'gs-netcat'
            - 'GSOCKET_ARGS'
    condition: selection_binary or selection_commandline
falsepositives:
    - Legitimate administrative or developer use of GSocket/gs-netcat for NAT-traversing shell access
    - Security testing, CTF work and lab environments where the tool is deliberately installed
level: medium
